<body>
  <Header />
  <main>
  <article class="container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <span class="tag">CRM</span>
      <h1>HubSpot vs Salesforce: Best CRM Platform 2026</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated August 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="lead">
      HubSpot and Salesforce are the two CRM platforms that every growing business shortlists in 2026, and the decision is rarely simple. HubSpot wins on ease of use, a genuinely free CRM tier, and unified marketing-sales-service tooling. Salesforce wins on customization depth, enterprise scalability, and the AppExchange ecosystem. This comparison breaks down pricing, ease of use, automation, integrations, reporting, and scalability so you can pick the right CRM for your team.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-top">
      <a href="https://www.hubspot.com" class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try HubSpot Free</a>
      <a href="https://www.salesforce.com" class="btn btn-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Salesforce Free</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Quick Comparison</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>HubSpot</th><th>Salesforce</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Best For</td><td>SMBs, marketing-led teams, fast onboarding</td><td>Enterprise, complex customization, RevOps</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Starting Price</td><td>$20/mo (Starter), free CRM available</td><td>$25/mo (Starter Suite), free suite for up to 2 users</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Free Tier</td><td>Yes, 1M contacts</td><td>Yes, up to 2 users (Free Suite)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ease of Use</td><td>Excellent, intuitive interface</td><td>Steep learning curve</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Customization</td><td>Moderate, guided</td><td>Extensive, code-level control</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Automation</td><td>Visual workflows, no code</td><td>Flow, Apex, complex logic</td></tr>
        <tr><td>App Ecosystem</td><td>1,500+ marketplace apps</td><td>7,000+ AppExchange apps</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Marketing Automation</td><td>Built-in Marketing Hub</td><td>Requires Marketing Cloud add-on</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reporting</td><td>Custom report builder, dashboards</td><td>Reports & Dashboards, Einstein Analytics</td></tr>
        <tr><td>AI Features</td><td>HubSpot AI, Copilot</td><td>Einstein AI, predictive analytics</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mobile App</td><td>iOS, Android, full featured</td><td>iOS, Android, full featured</td></tr>
        <tr><td>API Access</td><td>REST API, webhooks</td><td>REST, SOAP, Bulk, Streaming APIs</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Implementation Time</td><td>Days to weeks</td><td>Weeks to months</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Multi-Cloud Architecture</td><td>Unified platform</td><td>Sales, Service, Marketing Clouds</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Open Source</td><td>No, proprietary SaaS</td><td>No, proprietary SaaS</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What Is HubSpot?</h2>
    <p>HubSpot is an all-in-one CRM platform founded in 2006 by Brian Halligan and Dharmesh Shah, headquartered in Cambridge, Massachusetts. The company pioneered the concept of inbound marketing and built a platform designed to make marketing, sales, and service tools work together in a single system rather than as disconnected point solutions. HubSpot's core philosophy is that powerful software should be easy to use, which is why its interface is consistently rated the most intuitive among enterprise CRM platforms in 2026.</p>
    <p>The current pricing starts at $20 per month for the Starter plan, with a genuinely free CRM tier that supports up to 1 million contacts with no time limit. HubSpot organizes its product into five hubs: Marketing Hub, Sales Hub, Service Hub, Content Hub, and Operations Hub, each available standalone or bundled in the CRM Suite. Higher tiers add advanced automation, custom reporting, predictive lead scoring, and dedicated account management. The defining technical feature is that all hubs share a single database and contact record, which means marketing, sales, and service teams see the same data without synchronization. The tradeoff is that deep customization is more constrained than Salesforce, and pricing scales steeply once you move beyond Starter into Professional and Enterprise tiers.</p>

    <h2>What Is Salesforce?</h2>
    <p>Salesforce is the leading enterprise CRM platform, founded in 1999 by Marc Benioff and Parker Harris and headquartered in San Francisco, California. Salesforce pioneered the software-as-a-service model for CRM and has grown into the most widely used CRM in the world, powering over 150,000 companies across industries. Unlike HubSpot, which prioritizes ease of use, Salesforce is built for maximum flexibility and customization, allowing large organizations to model virtually any business process within the platform.</p>
    <p>The current pricing starts at $25 per month per user for the Starter Suite, and a Free Suite covers up to 2 users at no cost. Salesforce is organized into clouds, with Sales Cloud, Service Cloud, Marketing Cloud, Commerce Cloud, and Data Cloud as the primary products. Higher tiers add enterprise-grade features like custom objects, Apex code execution, Sandboxes for development, and unlimited sandboxes. Salesforce also offers Einstein AI for predictive analytics and the AppExchange marketplace with over 7,000 third-party applications. The defining technical feature is that Salesforce is a platform-as-a-service where you can build entire business applications using custom objects, flows, and Apex code. The tradeoff is that this power comes with a steep learning curve, longer implementation times, and the frequent need for certified administrators or consulting partners.</p>

    <h2>Ease of Use and Onboarding</h2>
    <p>Ease of use is the single biggest differentiator between HubSpot and Salesforce. HubSpot was designed for marketing and sales teams to use without IT support, while Salesforce was designed to be configured by administrators to fit complex business processes. Both philosophies are valid, but they lead to very different day-one experiences.</p>

    <h3>HubSpot Onboarding Experience</h3>
    <p>HubSpot is widely considered the most intuitive CRM on the market. The onboarding wizard walks you through connecting your email, importing contacts, creating deal pipelines, and setting up your first email campaign in under an hour. The interface uses plain language and contextual help, so a marketing manager with no CRM experience can build a functional workflow on day one. The contact record is clean and consolidated, showing every interaction a lead has had with your company across marketing, sales, and service in one timeline. HubSpot also offers a free CRM certification academy that trains teams on the platform at no cost.</p>
    <p>The tradeoff is that this simplicity comes with constraints. You cannot create custom objects on lower tiers, the data model is more rigid, and complex business logic that would take a few lines of Apex on Salesforce may be impossible in HubSpot without workarounds. For most small to mid-size teams, these constraints never matter. For teams with complex revenue operations, they become apparent quickly.</p>

    <h3>Salesforce Onboarding Experience</h3>
    <p>Salesforce has a steeper learning curve by design. The platform assumes you will configure it to match your business process, which means the out-of-the-box experience requires more setup. New users often work with a Salesforce administrator or a consulting partner to set up custom fields, page layouts, validation rules, and automation flows before the CRM feels usable. The interface is powerful but dense, with settings spread across Setup menus that can overwhelm first-time users.</p>
    <p>The tradeoff is that this initial investment pays off for complex organizations. Once configured, Salesforce can model virtually any business process, from multi-tier approval chains to territory management to revenue recognition rules. Salesforce Trailhead offers free interactive training modules, and the Trailblazer community is one of the largest in enterprise software. For organizations willing to invest in configuration, Salesforce becomes more powerful over time rather than more limiting.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for fast onboarding and intuitive design goes to <span class="winner-tag">HubSpot</span>. Best for configuration depth and complex business modeling goes to <span class="winner-tag">Salesforce</span>.</p>

    <h2>Automation and Workflows</h2>
    <p>Automation is where the philosophical split between HubSpot and Salesforce becomes most visible. HubSpot's workflows are visual and no-code, designed for marketers and sales ops teams to build without developer help. Salesforce Flow is more powerful and can handle complex logic, but it requires more expertise to build and maintain.</p>

    <h3>HubSpot Workflows</h3>
    <p>HubSpot's workflow builder uses a visual drag-and-drop interface where you define triggers, actions, and branches. Common use cases include lead scoring, lifecycle stage transitions, email nurturing sequences, task assignment, and property updates. Workflows can branch based on contact properties, list membership, form submissions, and engagement signals. The interface is forgiving, with undo history and test mode that lets you validate logic before activating a workflow. For marketing automation and sales sequence automation, HubSpot workflows cover the vast majority of needs without code.</p>
    <p>The limitation is that HubSpot workflows cannot handle complex cross-object logic, scheduled batches of custom Apex-style operations, or real-time API-triggered actions with the same flexibility as Salesforce. For teams whose automation needs are primarily marketing and sales cadence, HubSpot is faster and easier. For teams running revenue operations with approval chains and custom business logic, HubSpot hits a ceiling.</p>

    <h3>Salesforce Flow</h3>
    <p>Salesforce Flow is the automation engine that replaced workflow rules and process builder. Flow can handle record-triggered automation, scheduled actions, screen flows for guided user interactions, and auto-launched flows triggered by API calls or platform events. Flow supports complex branching, loops, and decision logic that can model multi-step approval processes, revenue split calculations, and cross-object field updates. For organizations with complex business processes, Flow is significantly more capable than HubSpot workflows.</p>
    <p>The tradeoff is that Flow has a steeper learning curve. Building a robust Flow requires understanding Salesforce's data model, governor limits, and best practices for bulkification. Many organizations hire certified Salesforce administrators or developers to build and maintain complex flows. Salesforce also supports Apex code for automation that exceeds Flow's capabilities, which is something HubSpot does not offer at all.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for marketing automation and no-code ease goes to <span class="winner-tag">HubSpot</span>. Best for complex business process automation and code-level control goes to <span class="winner-tag">Salesforce</span>.</p>

    <h2>Customization and Data Model</h2>
    <p>Customization is where Salesforce has a decisive advantage. Salesforce is a platform-as-a-service that lets you build entire business applications, while HubSpot is a productized CRM with guided customization. Both approaches serve different needs.</p>

    <h3>HubSpot Customization</h3>
    <p>HubSpot lets you create custom properties on contacts, companies, deals, and tickets, and on Enterprise tiers you can create custom objects. The interface guides you through setup, and most customization is done through configuration rather than code. HubSpot also offers calculated properties, association labels, and conditional property visibility. For most small to mid-size teams, this level of customization is sufficient to model their business.</p>
    <p>The limitation is that HubSpot's data model is more rigid. You cannot create arbitrary relationships between objects, governor limits cap API calls and workflow executions, and there is no equivalent to Apex for writing custom business logic. For teams that need a custom application built on top of CRM data, HubSpot is not the right platform.</p>

    <h3>Salesforce Customization</h3>
    <p>Salesforce lets you create custom objects, custom fields, custom relationships, and custom tabs, effectively allowing you to build a bespoke business application on top of the CRM. You can write Apex classes and triggers for custom business logic, build Visualforce or Lightning Web Components for custom interfaces, and use Sandboxes for development and testing. Salesforce also supports managed packages, which means third-party vendors build entire applications on the platform and distribute them through AppExchange.</p>
    <p>The tradeoff is that this power requires expertise. A complex Salesforce implementation typically involves a certified administrator, a developer, and sometimes an architect. The total cost of ownership for a heavily customized Salesforce org is significantly higher than HubSpot, but the platform can handle business complexity that HubSpot cannot model.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for guided, no-code customization goes to <span class="winner-tag">HubSpot</span>. Best for unlimited customization and platform-as-a-service goes to <span class="winner-tag">Salesforce</span>.</p>

    <h2>Integrations and App Ecosystem</h2>
    <p>The app ecosystem is where Salesforce has a structural advantage. Salesforce AppExchange has over 7,000 apps, while HubSpot's App Marketplace has around 1,500. However, HubSpot covers the most common integrations well, and its native connections between hubs reduce the need for third-party apps.</p>

    <h3>HubSpot App Marketplace</h3>
    <p>HubSpot's App Marketplace includes integrations with major tools like Slack, Zoom, Mailchimp, Shopify, Stripe, QuickBooks, and most marketing and sales tools. Apps install with a few clicks and are reviewed by HubSpot for quality. The advantage is that HubSpot's native hubs reduce the need for many integrations, because marketing, sales, service, and content tools are already unified. The disadvantage is that for niche industry tools or complex enterprise systems, the marketplace is thinner than Salesforce's.</p>

    <h3>Salesforce AppExchange</h3>
    <p>Salesforce AppExchange is the largest enterprise app marketplace in the world, with over 7,000 apps covering finance, healthcare, manufacturing, professional services, and virtually every industry. Many apps are built by ISVs as managed packages that extend Salesforce into a full business application, such as FinancialForce for ERP, Veeva for life sciences, and Service Cloud Voice for contact centers. The advantage is depth and industry specialization. The disadvantage is that many AppExchange apps are expensive and require implementation effort to configure.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for essential integrations and native hub unification goes to <span class="winner-tag">HubSpot</span>. Best for ecosystem depth and industry-specific apps goes to <span class="winner-tag">Salesforce</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pricing Comparison</h2>
    <p>Pricing is where HubSpot and Salesforce diverge based on team size and needs. HubSpot is cheaper at entry and small scale, while Salesforce becomes more predictable at mid-market and enterprise scale. The right choice depends on which hubs or clouds you need and how many seats you require.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>HubSpot</th><th>Salesforce</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Free</td><td>Free CRM, 1M contacts</td><td>Free Suite, up to 2 users</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Starter</td><td>$20/mo per user</td><td>$25/mo per user</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Professional</td><td>$100/mo per user (Sales Hub)</td><td>Pro Suite — $100/user/month</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Enterprise</td><td>$150/mo per user (Sales Hub)</td><td>$175/mo per user (Sales Cloud)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Marketing Pro</td><td>$890/mo (includes 2,000 contacts)</td><td>Marketing Cloud, custom pricing</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Implementation</td><td>Self-service or guided onboarding</td><td>Often requires consulting partner</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Free CRM Tier</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes, up to 2 users</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>For small teams under 10 people, HubSpot is significantly cheaper because the free CRM tier and $20 Starter plan cover most needs. Salesforce Starter at $25 per user per month is competitive on paper, and the Free Suite covers up to 2 users, but configuration needs mean the real cost of getting started is higher. One HubSpot cost detail to budget for: Sales Hub Professional and Enterprise each require a one-time onboarding fee ($1,500 and $3,500 respectively), and AI plus automation actions are metered through HubSpot Credits at $9 per 1,000 credits, with plans including 500 (Starter), 3,000 (Professional), and 5,000 (Enterprise) credits. For mid-market teams of 50 to 500 people, total cost of ownership becomes comparable, with HubSpot's Marketing Hub Professional at $890 per month offsetting Salesforce's lower per-seat costs. For enterprise teams above 500 people, Salesforce is often more predictable because its per-seat licensing is well understood, while HubSpot's contact-based marketing pricing can spike unexpectedly as your database grows.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for small teams and free entry goes to <span class="winner-tag">HubSpot</span>. Best for predictable per-seat scaling at enterprise goes to <span class="winner-tag">Salesforce</span>.</p>

    <h2>Reporting and Analytics</h2>
    <p>Both platforms offer robust reporting, but they take different approaches. HubSpot's reporting is designed for self-service by marketers and sales managers, while Salesforce's reporting is deeper and more customizable but requires more setup.</p>
    <p>HubSpot offers a custom report builder, dashboards, and attribution reporting on Professional and Enterprise tiers. Reports cover pipeline, revenue, marketing performance, and service metrics, and they pull from the unified database so cross-hub reporting works without data blending. HubSpot's reporting is generally considered easier to build and sufficient for most SMB needs.</p>
    <p>Salesforce offers Reports and Dashboards as the standard layer, with Einstein Analytics and CRM Analytics available as add-ons for advanced use cases. Salesforce reporting supports joined reports, cross-filter, custom summary formulas, and row-level formulas that can handle complex analytical questions. For organizations that live in data and need granular control over reporting, Salesforce is more capable. The tradeoff is that building complex Salesforce reports requires training, and many teams rely on a dedicated analyst.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for self-service reporting and ease goes to <span class="winner-tag">HubSpot</span>. Best for depth and complex analytics goes to <span class="winner-tag">Salesforce</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pros and Cons</h2>

    <h3>HubSpot Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Genuinely free CRM tier with 1 million contacts</li>
        <li>Most intuitive interface among enterprise CRMs</li>
        <li>Unified platform across marketing, sales, and service</li>
        <li>Visual, no-code workflow builder</li>
        <li>Fast onboarding, often usable within a day</li>
        <li>Strong native marketing automation in Marketing Hub</li>
        <li>Free Academy training and certification</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>HubSpot Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Pricing scales steeply beyond Starter tier</li>
        <li>Limited customization compared to Salesforce</li>
        <li>No code-level control or Apex equivalent</li>
        <li>Marketing Hub Professional at $890/mo is a price jump</li>
        <li>Contact-based pricing on marketing can spike as database grows</li>
        <li>Smaller app ecosystem at around 1,500 apps</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Salesforce Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Unlimited customization with custom objects and Apex</li>
        <li>AppExchange with over 7,000 apps</li>
        <li>Enterprise-grade scalability for thousands of users</li>
        <li>Powerful Flow automation for complex business processes</li>
        <li>Einstein AI for predictive analytics</li>
        <li>Industry-specific solutions and managed packages</li>
        <li>Deep API access with REST, SOAP, Bulk, and Streaming</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Salesforce Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Steep learning curve, often requires certified admin</li>
        <li>Free Suite capped at 2 users; paid plans start at $25/mo per user</li>
        <li>Longer implementation time, often weeks to months</li>
        <li>Marketing Cloud is a separate, expensive product</li>
        <li>Higher total cost of ownership with consulting partners</li>
        <li>Interface can overwhelm first-time users</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Who Should Use Which?</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Small Businesses and Startups</h3>
      <p>If you have under 50 employees and want a CRM running within a week, HubSpot is the better choice. The free CRM tier covers basic contact management, and the Starter plan at $20 per month adds email automation and deal pipelines. The intuitive interface means your team actually uses the CRM rather than abandoning it. Salesforce Starter works but requires more configuration, though its Free Suite gives very small teams and first-time CRM users a no-cost way in before committing to a paid tier. For more options, see our <a href="/reviews/best-crm-small-business-2026/">best CRM for small business</a> guide.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Marketing-Led Growth Teams</h3>
      <p>If your primary growth engine is inbound marketing, content, and lead nurturing, HubSpot is the stronger choice. Marketing Hub includes email marketing, landing pages, SEO tools, ad management, and social scheduling in one platform. Salesforce Marketing Cloud is powerful but is a separate product with separate pricing and a steeper learning curve. For marketing-first teams, HubSpot's unified platform reduces tool sprawl.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Enterprise Sales Organizations</h3>
      <p>If you have a dedicated RevOps team, complex sales processes, and more than 500 users, Salesforce is the standard. Custom objects let you model any data relationship, Flow handles complex automation, and AppExchange provides industry-specific extensions. Enterprise sales organizations with territory management, revenue split rules, and approval chains need Salesforce's flexibility, because HubSpot Enterprise hits customization limits.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Companies in Regulated Industries</h3>
      <p>If you operate in healthcare, finance, or government with strict compliance requirements, Salesforce has deeper compliance certifications and industry-specific clouds like Health Cloud and Financial Services Cloud. HubSpot is SOC 2 and GDPR compliant but lacks the vertical-specific compliance tooling that Salesforce offers. For regulated industries, Salesforce is the safer enterprise choice.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Teams Without a Dedicated Admin</h3>
      <p>If you do not have a Salesforce-certified administrator on staff or budget for a consulting partner, HubSpot is the better choice. HubSpot is designed to be managed by marketing and sales operations teams without deep technical skills. Salesforce can become difficult to maintain without an admin, and configuration drift can cause automation failures that are expensive to fix. If Salesforce sounds like too much admin overhead, our <a href="/reviews/salesforce-alternatives-2026/">Salesforce alternatives</a> roundup covers lighter options like Pipedrive and Zoho.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Real-World Use Cases</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Launching a 20-person SaaS startup's first CRM</h3>
      <p>A 20-person SaaS startup needs a CRM to track trials, demos, and closed-won deals. HubSpot Free CRM covers contact management, and the team upgrades to Sales Hub Starter at $20 per user per month for email sequences and meeting scheduling. The team is fully onboarded in three days and reports adoption above 90%. Salesforce Starter would work but require more configuration, though its Free Suite could cover a 2-person team at $0, making HubSpot the faster path to a working CRM for a 20-person company.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Scaling a 1,000-person enterprise with complex RevOps</h3>
      <p>A 1,000-person enterprise needs territory management, multi-currency revenue splits, approval chains for discounts above 15%, and integration with an ERP system. Salesforce Sales Cloud Enterprise at $175 per user per month, combined with custom objects and Flow, models the entire revenue process. A certified admin and a consulting partner configure the system over eight weeks. HubSpot Enterprise cannot model the approval chains or the ERP integration at the same depth, making Salesforce the necessary choice.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Running an inbound marketing campaign for a B2B agency</h3>
      <p>A B2B marketing agency wants to run a content-driven lead generation campaign with ebooks, webinars, and email nurturing sequences. HubSpot Marketing Hub Professional at $890 per month includes landing pages, email automation, lead scoring, and attribution reporting in one platform. The marketing team builds the campaign in a week without developer help. Salesforce Marketing Cloud could do the same but requires more setup and a separate license, making HubSpot the more efficient choice for marketing-led campaigns.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Managing a global sales team across five regions</h3>
      <p>A global company with sales teams in North America, EMEA, APAC, and LATAM needs multi-currency support, regional forecasting, and territory hierarchy. Salesforce Sales Cloud Enterprise with Enterprise Territory Management handles this natively, with role hierarchies and territory-based forecast rollups. HubSpot supports multiple currencies and teams but lacks the territory management depth that global sales operations require. For global multi-region sales, Salesforce is the stronger choice.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Final Verdict</h2>
    <div class="verdict">
      <p><strong>Best for small to mid-size teams and marketing-led growth: HubSpot.</strong> HubSpot's free CRM tier, intuitive interface, and unified marketing-sales-service platform make it the best choice for teams that want a working CRM fast without hiring an administrator. The Starter plan at $20 per month is competitive, and the platform scales smoothly through Professional and Enterprise for teams up to around 1,000 employees.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for enterprise and complex customization: Salesforce.</strong> Salesforce's custom objects, Apex code, Flow automation, and AppExchange ecosystem make it the best choice for organizations with complex revenue operations, dedicated RevOps teams, and needs that exceed what a productized CRM can model. At $25 per user per month for Starter, it is competitive on entry pricing, though total cost of ownership rises with configuration.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for marketing automation: HubSpot.</strong> HubSpot Marketing Hub includes email marketing, lead scoring, attribution, and content tools in one platform, while Salesforce Marketing Cloud is a separate and more expensive product. For marketing-first teams, HubSpot reduces tool sprawl and is easier to manage without specialized expertise.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended approach:</strong> Start on HubSpot if you are a small to mid-size team, want fast onboarding, or prioritize marketing automation. Start on Salesforce if you have a dedicated admin, complex business processes, or are scaling past 500 users with RevOps needs. Many companies run both platforms together using the native integration, using Salesforce as the sales system of record and HubSpot for marketing automation. Most teams pick the right platform within the first 90 days of implementation.</p>
    </div>

