    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is HubSpot better than Salesforce for small businesses?</h3>
    <p>Yes, HubSpot is better for most small businesses because it offers a genuinely free CRM tier, a more intuitive interface, and faster onboarding. Salesforce requires more configuration, often needs a certified administrator, and its Starter plan lacks the polish of HubSpot at the same price point. For a team under 50 people that wants a working CRM in a week, HubSpot wins. Salesforce becomes more attractive once you need deep customization, complex automation, or have a dedicated admin.</p>

    <h3>Which is cheaper, HubSpot or Salesforce?</h3>
    <p>At the entry level, HubSpot is cheaper because its CRM is free for up to 1 million contacts, and the Starter plan begins at $20 per month. Salesforce Starter begins at $25 per month, and a Free Suite covers up to 2 users. However, HubSpot's cost climbs steeply once you add Marketing Hub Professional at $890 per month or Enterprise tiers. Salesforce also gets expensive at scale, but its per-seat pricing on Sales Cloud is more predictable. For small teams, HubSpot is cheaper. For mid-market teams with complex needs, total cost of ownership is comparable.</p>

    <h3>Which CRM has better automation, HubSpot or Salesforce?</h3>
    <p>Salesforce has more powerful automation through Flow, which can handle complex multi-branch logic, scheduled actions, and apex-triggered workflows. HubSpot's workflows are easier to build visually and cover most marketing and sales automation needs without code. For marketing automation like email sequences, lead scoring, and lifecycle stage updates, HubSpot is faster to set up. For complex business processes like approval chains, revenue operations, and custom object automation, Salesforce Flow is more capable but requires more expertise.</p>

    <h3>Can I migrate from HubSpot to Salesforce?</h3>
    <p>Yes, you can migrate from HubSpot to Salesforce. Salesforce offers a Data Import Wizard and tools like Data Loader that transfer contacts, companies, deals, and activities from HubSpot. The process typically takes a few days for databases under 50,000 records. You will need to map HubSpot properties to Salesforce fields, rebuild workflows in Flow, and migrate email templates and sequences manually. Many companies hire a Salesforce consulting partner for migrations above 100,000 records or complex custom objects.</p>

    <h3>Which CRM scales better for enterprise, HubSpot or Salesforce?</h3>
    <p>Salesforce scales better for large enterprises with complex needs. Its custom objects, Apex code, AppExchange ecosystem with over 7,000 apps, and multi-org architecture support global companies with thousands of users and complex revenue operations. HubSpot Enterprise is strong for marketing-led companies up to around 1,000 employees but hits limits on custom data models, complex permission hierarchies, and deep API-driven automation. For enterprises above 1,000 employees with dedicated RevOps teams, Salesforce is the standard.</p>

    <h3>Can I use HubSpot and Salesforce together?</h3>
    <p>Yes, and many companies do. HubSpot offers a native Salesforce integration that syncs contacts, companies, deals, and activities bidirectionally between the two platforms. A common setup is to use Salesforce as the system of record for sales and revenue operations while using HubSpot for marketing automation, lead nurturing, and content management. The integration requires configuration on both sides and works best when you define clear ownership rules to avoid data conflicts. Combined cost is higher, but the two tools complement each other well.</p>

